WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.758
CVA6 executes instructions, while the platform connects memory, interrupts,

2
00:00:07.758 --> 00:00:10.034
debug and peripherals.

3
00:00:10.034 --> 00:00:17.999
The toolchain compiles and links binaries matching the ISA, ABI and platform;

4
00:00:17.999 --> 00:00:22.964
it is a build tool, not a runtime handoff stage.

5
00:00:22.964 --> 00:00:30.825
Bare-metal, RTOS and conventional Linux environments need different support.

6
00:00:30.825 --> 00:00:37.549
In the pinned SDK Genesys2 example, BootROM loads later software,

7
00:00:37.549 --> 00:00:43.652
OpenSBI supplies machine-mode services, U-Boot loads Linux,

8
00:00:43.652 --> 00:00:47.066
and the kernel starts user space.

9
00:00:47.066 --> 00:00:53.375
This is a control-handoff sequence: OpenSBI remains resident.

10
00:00:53.375 --> 00:00:59.685
The QEMU route skips U-Boot and is not CVA6 RTL verification.

11
00:00:59.685 --> 00:01:08.581
The dts label denotes device-tree source; the kernel normally receives a compiled DTB.

12
00:01:08.581 --> 00:01:13.443
The ext4 label is a generic filesystem example.

13
00:01:13.443 --> 00:01:21.615
This SDK FIT actually packages u-boot.dtb and a rootfs.cpio.gz initial ramdisk,

14
00:01:21.615 --> 00:01:26.270
so ext4 must not be described as its default.

15
00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:34.338
The maintainer-listed targets are cv32a6_ima_sv32_fpga and cv64a6_imafdc_sv39;

16
00:01:34.338 --> 00:01:39.407
this course has not rerun their boot experiments.

17
00:01:39.407 --> 00:01:44.786
Hardware icons and connections describe composition.

18
00:01:44.786 --> 00:01:51.095
DDR, AXI, PLIC and JTAG are examples, not universal features.

19
00:01:51.095 --> 00:01:57.612
Copying a Linux image cannot establish support for a new board;

20
00:01:57.612 --> 00:02:05.060
configuration and dependency changes still require compatibility checks.

